 2. Hot water Hegseth 
 
 By Colin Demarest and Thomas Novelly 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Allie Carl/Axios. Photo: Kevin Dietsch/Getty Images &amp; PhotoQuest via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Tuesday's election was great for Democrats. It was bad for Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and some of the White House's most ambitious national security projects.

 Why it matters: Surprise wins across the map for Democrats — including in the Deep South — complicate a lot for the Trump administration.

 Republicans have relied on a simple-majority funding mechanism known as reconciliation to pay for marquee projects like Golden Dome . That option is now a long shot over the next few months, and impossible after that. Outrage over the Iran war, well beyond the half-year mark, is sure to turbocharge a long list of congressional inquiries. And lawmakers on both sides of the aisle, including Reps. Pat Ryan (D-N.Y.) and Thomas Massie (R-Ky.), have suggested impeaching Hegseth. (My colleagues Marc Caputo and Alex Isenstadt report that Hegseth is on "thin ice.") Follow the money: Defense analysts agree prospects for the administration's full $1.5 trillion defense budget look grim.

 A Wednesday note from TD Cowen's Washington Research Group warned that "Trump 'branded' projects are dead." Golden Dome, Golden Fleet and "other signature efforts," it added, "will be curbed." Meanwhile, Todd Harrison, an American Enterprise Institute defense budget expert, said it's unlikely the lame-duck congressional session will pass reconciliation and that it instead will keep the defense budget operating under a continuing resolution into next year. "They're all at risk," Harrison said of reconciliation-backed programs. "My base expectation is nothing happens on the defense budget." Read more from Colin and me here . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 3. Space Force SOCOM 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Chief Master Sgt. Charles Apodaca unfurls the U.S. Space Forces Special Operations Command flag. Photo: Marleah Miller / DVIDS

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Specially trained, space-focused troops are needed for future special operations missions, a top commander told reporters.

 Why it matters: The Space Force is becoming more crucial for the U.S. military's missions, including operations with its most elite units.

 Space Forces Special Operations Command was formally activated this week at MacDill Air Force Base in Tampa, Florida. Col. Peter "Charlie" Norsky, the command's new leader, said it will plan with those highly trained Guardians in mind for future fights. What he's saying: Norsky said Guardians in a "space special warfare team" can help the military's top operators identify vulnerabilities such as electromagnetic threats, GPS jamming and interference with battlefield communications.

 "For us, it's about preserving survivability, awareness and [speed of decision] when every second matters," said. Catch up quick: Axios first reported the stand-up of the new command last month.

 It follows the creation of components focused on space threats in the Middle East, Europe, Africa and the Pacific. The bottom line: The Space Force wants brains and brawn for its special operations.

 "We're looking for those Guardians who are expert at their craft, whether that's operations, intel, cyber, and that they are able to maneuver with their [special operations] counterparts," Norsky said. 
 
